---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Display from '../components/Display.astro';

/**
 * Three moves, two stories each. The order is the argument: change the language
 * decisions are made in, encode the standard they must satisfy, then grow the
 * people who can operate there.
 */
const sections = [
  {
    title: 'Change the planning conversation',
    intro:
      'Design cannot influence strategy if its reasoning becomes legible only after priorities have been set. I gave teams shared ways to connect user needs with business value and to expose the cost of complexity before either became expensive to change.',
    conclusion:
      'Design moved upstream when it improved the quality of the decision — not merely the presentation of the proposal.',
    cards: [
      {
        lever: 'Value',
        title: 'Give design a language the business can use',
        summary:
          'Design proposals were being judged as visual improvements because the value behind them remained implicit. I introduced a taxonomy of work, a map to business metrics, and a rubric for evaluating user value, business value, implementation effort, and risk.',
        evidence: 'Executives began referencing the resulting design levers in funding meetings.',
        link: 'Three models that tied design to business metrics',
        href: '/how-i-translated-design-work',
        fig: '/work/essays/how-i-translated-design-work/fig-2.svg',
        alt: 'Two-by-two placing the four business levers: efficiency in operation and now, growth in operation and future, retention in customer and now, strategic alignment in customer and future.',
      },
      {
        lever: 'Scope',
        title: 'Make complexity part of the cost',
        summary:
          'Teams agreed that simplicity mattered but had no shared way to defend it. I introduced a complexity budget that measured decision, attention, and orientation load — giving product, research, design, and engineering a common basis for reducing scope before overbuild reached the interface.',
        evidence: 'The conversation moved from polishing complexity to questioning whether it belonged.',
        link: 'A complexity budget for scope decisions',
        href: '/how-i-managed-ux-complexity',
        fig: '/work/essays/how-i-managed-ux-complexity/fig-2.svg',
        alt: 'Heat map scoring four product flows against decision, attention and orientation load, the heaviest cells shaded darkest.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Turn standards into shared infrastructure',
    intro:
      'A standard does not scale because a leader declares it. It scales when other teams find it useful enough to depend on. I built systems that clarified how work should be made and where continued investment stopped returning value.',
    conclusion:
      'Quality became easier to hold when the standard existed outside any one review or reviewer.',
    cards: [
      {
        lever: 'Coherence',
        title: 'Build a system teams choose to adopt',
        summary:
          'Product quality was drifting across siloed teams, conflicting standards, and repeated handoff failures. I led a global design system of more than 200 components that became a shared operating layer across product, engineering, creative, marketing, and planning.',
        evidence:
          'Adoption spread through usefulness rather than mandate. Product managers learned the system, engineering mirrored its foundations, and teams began planning around its availability.',
        link: 'A design system five teams chose to adopt',
        href: '/how-i-scaled-design-efficiency',
        fig: '/work/essays/how-i-scaled-design-efficiency/fig-1.svg',
        alt: 'Three layers of design-system trust: shared language produces alignment, shared structure produces consistency, shared infrastructure produces velocity.',
      },
      {
        lever: 'Restraint',
        title: 'Establish a defensible stopping point',
        summary:
          'Teams rushed through problem definition, then pursued perfection during execution. I reframed “good enough” as a deliberate double standard: a threshold to exceed while defining the problem and one to defend once the work had delivered its intended value.',
        evidence:
          'Designers gained both permission and an obligation to challenge assumptions, align on success, and stop building when additional work no longer improved the outcome.',
        link: 'Knowing when to stop building',
        href: '/how-i-built-culture-around-paradox',
        fig: '/work/essays/how-i-built-culture-around-paradox/fig-1.svg',
        alt: 'Before and after table on team decision maturity: problem framing, alignment on success and knowing when to stop each move from implicit to deliberate.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Grow designers into decision-makers',
    intro:
      'Moving design upstream is meaningless if only the design leader is allowed to operate there. I changed roles, ownership, and advancement so designers could carry work from framing through delivery — and grow by influencing direction rather than merely increasing output.',
    conclusion:
      'The team’s influence grew because judgment became something designers could practice, demonstrate, and be rewarded for.',
    cards: [
      {
        lever: 'Ownership',
        title: 'Replace specialist handoffs with product ownership',
        summary:
          'After the pandemic reshaped the business and reduced the team, a specialist structure built for long, orchestrated initiatives became too brittle. I rebuilt the group around junior product-design generalists and coached them to own the complete process.',
        evidence:
          'Junior designers delivered work previously handled by senior specialists with greater speed and fewer dependencies, then grew into cross-functional planning and mentorship.',
        link: 'Rebuilding the team from specialists to generalists',
        href: '/how-i-unlocked-team-trust',
        fig: '/work/essays/how-i-unlocked-team-trust/fig-1.svg',
        alt: 'Before and after diagram of the team’s shape: separate specialist groups become one ring of product-design generalists around the product.',
      },
      {
        lever: 'Growth',
        title: 'Promote designers for the direction they shape',
        summary:
          'Execution skill dominated the career path, leaving capable designers outside strategic conversations. I introduced a development model that rewarded problem framing, recommendations, strategic influence, systems advocacy, and mentorship.',
        evidence:
          'Designers practiced strategy in roadmap and tradeoff conversations — not only in critique — and advancement became connected to visible planning impact.',
        link: 'Promoting designers for influence, not output',
        href: '/how-i-shaped-cross-functional-reputation',
        fig: '/work/essays/how-i-shaped-cross-functional-reputation/fig-1.svg',
        alt: 'A five-stage growth loop running from structure to trust to opportunity to visibility to advancement, and back.',
      },
    ],
  },
];

/** The organizational sequence the three sections follow. */
const progression = [
  ['Planning', 'Change the language used to make decisions'],
  ['Standards', 'Encode the standard those decisions must satisfy'],
  ['People', 'Develop people capable of applying and improving it'],
];

const strip = [
  'Executives used design’s language in funding meetings',
  'Five functions adopted a shared design system',
  'Junior generalists took ownership from framing through delivery',
  'Career growth became tied to influence, not output',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="I build design teams that change what gets decided"
  metaTitle="I build design teams that change what gets decided | Tyler Young"
  description="Getting design invited earlier was not the goal. Six stories about moving design upstream by changing how the company framed value, controlled scope, held standards, and developed judgment."
  current="essays"
>
  <header class="shell hero">
    <p class="label">How I lead</p>
    <Display text="I build design teams that change what gets decided">
      I build design teams that change what gets decided
    </Display>
    <p class="stand">
      Getting design invited earlier was not the goal. I wanted designers to
      improve the decisions being made there — what deserved investment, how much
      complexity users should absorb, when the work was ready, and what quality
      meant across teams.
    </p>
    <p class="open">
      That required more than advocating for design. I introduced shared ways to
      frame value, control scope, establish standards, and develop judgment. Some
      became planning instruments. Some changed the shape of the team. Together,
      they moved design from receiving decisions to helping make them. These six
      stories show how.
    </p>

    <ul class="strip">
      {strip.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <ol class="progression" aria-label="The sequence the three sections follow">
      {
        progression.map(([step, what], i) => (
          <li>
            <span class="prog-step">{step}</span>
            <span class="prog-what">{what}</span>
            {i < progression.length - 1 && <span class="prog-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </header>

  {
    sections.map((sec, si) => (
      <section class="shell block" aria-labelledby={`sec-${si}`}>
        <h2 class="heading sec-title" id={`sec-${si}`}>{sec.title}</h2>
        <p class="sec-intro">{sec.intro}</p>

        {sec.cards.map((c, ci) => (
          <article class:list={['card', { flip: (si * 2 + ci) % 2 === 1 }]}>
            <a class="media" href={c.href} tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
              <img src={c.fig} alt={c.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            </a>
            <div class="body">
              <p class="lever">{c.lever}</p>
              <h3 class="heading card-title">{c.title}</h3>
              <p class="summary">{c.summary}</p>
              <p class="evidence">{c.evidence}</p>
              <a class="essay-link" href={c.href}>
                {c.link} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </a>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}

        <p class="sec-close">{sec.conclusion}</p>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <section class="shell closing">
    <h2 class="heading">The point was never to put design into every meeting</h2>
    <p>It was to make design useful at the moments when product direction was still fluid.</p>
    <p>
      The business-value models changed how opportunities were framed. The
      complexity budget changed how scope was negotiated. The design system
      changed how quality traveled across teams. The “good enough” threshold
      changed when people challenged and when they committed. The role and career
      models changed who was equipped and expected to participate.
    </p>
    <p>
      My job as a design leader is not to make every consequential decision
      myself. It is to build a team, a language, and an operating environment in
      which better decisions become more likely — with or without me in the room.
    </p>
    <p class="close-line">
      I scale design by increasing the quality of the decisions the organization
      can make.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: 96px;
  }

  .stand {
    margin: 26px 0 0;
    max-width: 62ch;
    font-size: var(--text-lede);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .open {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--text-note);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  /* The four outcomes, banded like every parallel row on the site. */
  .strip {
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    margin: 40px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .strip li {
    padding: 16px 18px;
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .strip li:nth-child(odd) {
    background: var(--color-row);
  }

  /* Planning → Standards → People. A line, not a diagram. */
  .progression {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px 18px;
    margin: 40px 0 0;
    padding: 22px 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  .progression li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .prog-step {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .prog-what {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .prog-arrow {
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin-left: 8px;
  }

  .block {
    padding-top: 110px;
  }

  .sec-title {
    font-size: clamp(26px, 3.4vw, 34px);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .sec-intro {
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    font-size: var(--text-note);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  /* An essay card: evidence on one side, the case on the other. */
  .card {
    display: grid;
    gap: 26px;
    margin-top: 54px;
  }

  .media {
    display: block;
    background: var(--color-paper-raised);
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: 22px;
    align-self: start;
  }

  .media img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .lever {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .card-title {
    font-size: 23px;
    margin: 8px 0 0;
  }

  .summary {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-note);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  /* The one concrete consequence, set apart from the description. */
  .evidence {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-note);
    line-height: 1.5;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Ink, not blue: these sit six to a page, and a page of blue headlines reads
     as a link farm. The arrow does the pointing, as in the footer. */
  .essay-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 18px;
    font-size: 17px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 120ms ease;
  }

  .essay-link:hover,
  .essay-link:focus-visible {
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .sec-close {
    margin: 54px 0 0;
    max-width: 62ch;
    font-size: 19px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .closing {
    padding-top: 120px;
    padding-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .closing h2 {
    font-size: clamp(26px, 3.4vw, 34px);
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  .closing p {
    max-width: 66ch;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-size: var(--text-note);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  .close-line {
    font-size: 19px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-ink) !important;
    margin-top: 26px !important;
  }

  @media (min-width: 860px) {
    .strip {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .card {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 6fr) minmax(0, 5fr);
      gap: 48px;
      align-items: start;
    }

    /* Alternate sides so the page has a gait rather than a march. */
    .card.flip .media {
      order: 2;
    }
  }
</style>
